---
import AwsServiceIcon from './AwsServiceIcon.astro';
---
<figure class="visual">
        <figcaption class="visual-cap"><span class="label">Example · deploy a TCP service</span><h3>Use NLB for a TCP service with a stable zonal address</h3><p>The client connects to a listener, the listener forwards to a target group, and NLB selects a healthy target for that connection. It does not read an HTTP hostname or URL path.</p></figcaption>
        <div class="nlb-flow">
          <div class="nlb-path" aria-label="Client connection goes to an NLB listener, then a target group, then a healthy target">
            <article class="nlb-client"><span class="step-number">1</span><small>Client opens a connection</small><strong>203.0.113.24:51514</strong><code>TCP → 198.51.100.10:443</code></article>
            <div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
            <article class="nlb-entry"><div class="nlb-title"><span class="step-number">2</span><AwsServiceIcon service="nlb" size={38} /><div><small>Network Load Balancer</small><strong>payments-nlb</strong></div></div><dl><div><dt>Zonal address</dt><dd>198.51.100.10</dd></div><div><dt>Listener</dt><dd>TCP :443</dd></div><div><dt>Forwards to</dt><dd>tcp-service-tg</dd></div></dl><p>Reads the transport flow—not <code>Host</code> or <code>/checkout</code>.</p></article>
            <div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
            <article class="nlb-target-group"><span class="step-number">3</span><small>Target group · TCP :8443</small><strong>tcp-service-tg</strong><div class="nlb-target healthy"><span>selected</span><code>10.0.21.18:8443</code><b>HEALTHY</b></div><div class="nlb-target unhealthy"><span>excluded</span><code>10.0.22.41:8443</code><b>UNHEALTHY</b></div></article>
          </div>
          <div class="nlb-fit"><strong>Why NLB fits:</strong> the service only needs TCP forwarding and a stable address in this Availability Zone. There is no HTTP rule for NLB to evaluate.</div>
          <section class="connection-mapping" aria-labelledby="connection-mapping-title"><div><h4 id="connection-mapping-title">What stays together?</h4><p>NLB maps a transport connection to a target. AWS does not publish the target-selection formula.</p></div><article><strong>Existing connection</strong><code>source port 51514</code><p>Packets 1, 2, and 3 continue to <b>10.0.21.18:8443</b>.</p></article><article><strong>New connection</strong><code>source port 51515</code><p>Selection runs again and may choose another healthy target.</p></article></section>
          <div class="tls-modes"><article class="tls-mode"><strong>TCP listener :443</strong><code>encrypted bytes → target</code><p>The target can terminate TLS. NLB does not need to decrypt the HTTP request.</p></article><article class="tls-mode"><strong>TLS listener :443</strong><code>TLS ends at NLB → backend connection</code><p>NLB uses the configured target-group protocol for the target-side connection.</p></article></div>
          <div class="qualified"><strong>Client address is conditional.</strong> Instance targets and several protocol combinations preserve it by default. TCP/TLS IP targets have different defaults and use <code>preserve_client_ip.enabled</code>; topology restrictions also apply.</div>
        </div>
        <section class="nlb-integrations" aria-labelledby="nlb-integrations-title">
          <header><span class="label">Common compositions</span><h4 id="nlb-integrations-title">Follow the arrows: NLB, ALB, and VPC endpoints</h4><p>These patterns solve different problems. The target-group type tells you what NLB can actually forward to.</p></header>
          <div class="nlb-integration-grid">
            <article><div class="mini-path"><span><AwsServiceIcon service="nlb" size={32} />NLB</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><code>target type: alb</code><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span><AwsServiceIcon service="alb" size={32} />ALB</span></div><h5>NLB → ALB is a direct target type</h5><p>Use it when the same entry needs NLB features—such as zonal static addresses or PrivateLink—and ALB still needs to route HTTP requests.</p><small><strong>Configure:</strong> TCP target group of type <code>alb</code>; one ALB per target group; matching ports. Both load balancers must be in the same VPC and account, and the hop uses IPv4.</small></article>
            <article><div class="mini-path wide"><span><AwsServiceIcon service="vpce" size={32} />Interface VPCE</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><code>endpoint service</code><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span><AwsServiceIcon service="nlb" size={32} />NLB</span></div><h5>PrivateLink normally flows VPCE → NLB</h5><p>Use this to publish your service privately to consumer VPCs. The consumer creates an interface endpoint; your endpoint service is associated with the provider NLB.</p><small><strong>Do not model this as NLB → VPCE.</strong> For consumer traffic, the endpoint is the entrance and NLB is on the provider side.</small></article>
            <article><div class="mini-path wide"><span><AwsServiceIcon service="nlb" size={32} />NLB</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><code>target type: ip</code><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span><AwsServiceIcon service="vpce" size={32} />VPCE ENI IPs</span></div><h5>Literal NLB → VPCE is an IP-target pattern</h5><p>Use this only for a specific interface-endpoint bridge where the endpoint service and protocol support the path. NLB has no <code>vpce</code> target type.</p><small><strong>You own the plumbing:</strong> register reachable endpoint ENI private IPs, keep registrations aligned with endpoint lifecycle, and verify health checks, security groups, TLS hostname/SNI, and Availability Zone coverage.</small></article>
          </div>
        </section>
        <div class="service-details"><article><h4>Listener and target protocols</h4><p>Listeners support TCP, TLS, UDP, TCP_UDP, QUIC, and TCP_QUIC. Target-group combinations vary by listener protocol; only a TCP listener can forward to an ALB target.</p></article><article><h4>Flow and connection controls</h4><p>Flow mapping uses transport fields. Configure cross-zone load balancing, deregistration delay, source-IP stickiness where supported, Proxy Protocol v2, and client-IP preservation with care.</p></article><article><h4>Network and operations</h4><p>Security groups can filter NLB traffic when attached. Observe healthy targets, zonal health, active and new flows, reset counts, processed bytes, port allocation errors, and TLS negotiation errors.</p></article></div>
        <div class="behavior-note"><strong>Behavior to keep in mind.</strong> If all registered targets are unhealthy, NLB can fail open to them. DNS may remove an unhealthy zone before its cached address expires. Existing transport connections are not automatically recreated on another target.</div>
        <div class="takeaway"><strong>Configure it this way:</strong> create the client-facing listener, forward it to a compatible target group, register healthy targets in every enabled zone, and plan for one NLB address per zone.</div>
      </figure>
